      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247822/assets/0cf50074bd5b43815dd03e88db20086dcdefb42f4e01d30e37be014fa80d772f.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n- The point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- Its range is $y\\in [0,\\infty )$\n\n- The function is increasing over its entire domain.\n\n- As $x\\to \\infty $, we have $y\\to \\infty ,$ though it does so slowly\n\nOf the given options, only graph II has all of the above properties. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
